          %title
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            How do you type check
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} MILL
            = succeed "?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            %a{:href => "#Shulman"} Mike Shulman
            gave one such system, but its type-checking algorithm requires two passes (if this algorithm were incorporated into the rules, there would be an extra intermediate judgment with “activeness” annotations on the types), and doesn’t handle additional type constructors such as
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+implication"} linear implication
          %p This page is intended to give algorithmic rules for a system with tensor projections and linear implication, but at present it is unverified and its interpretation into monoidal categories is unclear.
          %h2#judgment_form_notations Judgment form, Notations
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi t
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi T
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊠
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi U
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq \Gamma \vdash t\,:\,T\,\boxtimes\,U' \subseteq \Gamma'
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            are inputs;
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U'
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma'
            are outputs.
          %p
            The observation mentioned in
            %a{:href => "#Allais"} Allais
            motivating the use of “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊠
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \boxtimes
            ” was that a term generally doesn’t use all the resources it’s given, so the rest are passed back out, tensored with the result type.
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma'
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contexts"} contexts
            with some natural number of semicolon “scope” delimiters.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U'
            are “usage” sets, which are subsets of the (domains of the) respective contexts. In
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Allais"} Allais
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Allais"} Allais
            %em different
            contexts, because type checking a term generally adds projections to the context as potentially-consumed resources.
          %p The purpose of delimited scopes in the context is to remember by when we must’ve consumed tensor projections. Currently, only lambda adds a scope delimiter. The current algorithm puts projections in the outermost scope it can get away with. This approach will not work when adding additive connectives.
          %p
            In the rules, I implicitly coerce a context to the usage set of its entire domain. The abbreviation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Gamma \mapsto \Gamma')U
            “weakens”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            to include all entries in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma'
            but not
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi U
                    %mo ≔
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∪
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ∖
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Gamma \mapsto \Gamma')U \coloneqq U \cup (\Gamma' \setminus \Gamma)
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Δ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta
            = succeed "." do
              %em without any delimiters
          %p
            Some important (intended) properties of the judgment form are that if
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi t
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi T
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi U
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq \Gamma \vdash t\,:\,T\,\boxtimes\,U' \subseteq \Gamma'
          %ul
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq \Gamma
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U' \subseteq \Gamma'
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma'
              have the same number of delimiters.
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma'
              includes all the entries of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              = succeed ".)" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ′
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \subseteq \Gamma'
            %li
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ′
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ↦
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U' \subseteq (\Gamma \mapsto \Gamma')U
          %p
            Note that the context grows while the usage shrinks. Erm, I guess these usage sets are the sets of resources
            %em not
            used (and thus available).
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Gamma \mapsto \Gamma')U
            should be understood as the
            %em actual
            usage indicated by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊆
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subseteq \Gamma
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ′
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma'
          %h2#generators_and_functions Generators and Functions
          %p
            There are generator morphisms, which can be used any number of times.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi 𝒢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ;
                  %mover
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \in \mathcal{G}(A;\overrightarrow{B})
            means you can get
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊸
                  %mover
                    %mi B
                    %mo →
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\,:\,A \multimap \overrightarrow{B}
            whenever you want.
          %p
            Functions take exactly one argument, and have one or more results. Semantically, the results are tensored (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            ) together. Following
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Shulman"} Shulman
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(a)
            has two results, they are typically written as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{(1)}(a)
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 2
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_{(2)}(a)
            %em both
            results is a
            %em single
            use of linear implication elimination. This is actually the only way to use tensor projections, currently. Combining it with function application was purely a matter of convenience, and is probably a bad idea, for adding more connectives.
          %h2#rules Rules
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                      %mo :
                      %mi T
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi U
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi t
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo :
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi T
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ⊠
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mi U
                      %mo ∖
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{t:T \in U}{U \subseteq \Gamma \vdash t\,:\,T\,\boxtimes\,U \setminus \{t\} \subseteq \Gamma}
          %p
            Yes, you get
            %em terms
            out of the context, not just variables. For now, the only terms that end up in the context are variables and tensor projections.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
