          %p
            Whew. These rules add tensor projections of a function application to the context, the first time the application is encountered in a given scope. There are two rules because if the application doesn’t consume any resources, there’s generally an ambiguity between multiple instances of the application, so we add a “label” to disambiguate.
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi u
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} u
            is the label metavariable. This labeling idea is from
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Shulman"} Shulman
          %p
            In the rule mentioning
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_d
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_d
            is the set of resources consumed by the application. That is, both the function and argument. The premise
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi d
                  %mo ∩
                  %mi Δ
                  %mo ≠
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_d \cap \Delta \neq \emptyset
