            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_d
            is nonempty. Meanwhile, the other rule checks that the same computed usage
            %em is
            empty.
          %p
            In both rules,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Γ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma_2
            is split to find the scope to which the projections are added. If the application uses resources, that scope is the innermost scope with resources that were used. Otherwise it’s the outermost scope.
          %p The projections cannot go in a scope outside one with resources used, because then there would be no way to get the variables out there in ordinary MILL using a match.
          %p
            On the other hand, it can be trouble to put the projections in a scope more inside than necessary, since the lambda rule checks that everything in its scope was consumed. The projections only need to be consumed before one of their dependencies goes out of scope. For example, (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
